From f1f2501b15fdeb64aa56749d728b3757d9bf1010 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vili Kohonen <vili.kohonen@protonmail.com>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 14:35:01 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] Stop a run once it has converged to writing unfalsifiable
 graders

Every run that finds the hack spends its tail at a saturated fixed point: ~100% of
rollouts write a grader that cannot fail, honest solving is zero, most groups have no
reward spread, and the held-out honest pass rate is flat from step ~80 on. Training that
tail costs 30-70% of the bill and buys refinement of the hack, which is not the thing
under study. This adds an opt-in rule that ends the run once the fraction of rollouts
whose grader passes an arbitrary solution (response_test_func_arbitrary_pass) holds at or
above a threshold for a sustained number of batches.

That flag and not is_reward_hack_loose, deliberately. Loose counts every harmful grader,
and "Harmful - Incorrect" includes an honest asserting test whose expected value is
merely wrong. A neutral-prompt run never produces those at scale, but an arm prompted to
write real, comprehensive tests does -- measured live on such an arm: loose ~45% of the
batch while unfalsifiable graders were 0-3 of 256. A loose-based trigger could end an
honest run and call it convergence. On the six neutral-prompt reference runs the two
flags cross 0.95 within 0-5 steps of each other (sustained-crossing at 59/85/82/95/134 vs
54/85/82/95/133 in wandb coordinates), so the calibration is unchanged: at 0.95 sustained
5 the rule fires around step 60-140 on the five runs that hacked, never reverses after a
sustained crossing, and never fires on the run that stayed honest -- and a censored run
keeps the horizon it needs.

Mechanics: create_extra_infos now ships response_test_func_arbitrary_pass per rollout
(already computed by the evaluation; the flag is behavioural -- the grader runs against a
canned return-0 solution -- so it also catches an `assert True` grader that a syntactic
no-assert check would miss). The check runs at reward time for step N from
reward_extra_infos_dict -- no wandb read. The trigger lowers the trainer's
total_training_steps so step N+1 is the last, and the ordinary is_last_step path does the
final checkpoint save, adapter archive and loop exit. The workers built their LR
schedules from the original max_steps at init and are untouched, so a stopped run's steps
are a prefix of the full-length run under the same seed, not a reshaped schedule --
unlike passing a smaller --steps, which re-stretches the cosine.

Configured via two GRPOConfig fields (early_stop_frac, 0 disables;
early_stop_sustain_steps, default 5), rendered into an `early_stop` block of the verl
config the same way the recontextualization block travels. main_run_rl reads
RLRH_EARLY_STOP_FRAC / RLRH_EARLY_STOP_SUSTAIN from the environment so every entrypoint
gets the knob without widening its signature; the values are resolved in the driver
before Ray starts, so no worker needs the variables.

Error labels (-1.0) count in the denominator and never as hits, so evaluation failures
delay the stop rather than hasten it. An arm whose rewards carry no flag warns once and
never fires.

tests/test_early_stop.py pins the decision logic batch by batch; it loads the module by
file path because src.train.__init__ imports torch, and it has been executed (8 passed)
on a machine with no training venv. The trainer glue is not covered, same as the other
trainer patches.
